    "narrative": "Our primary offensive engine today features **Lawrence Butler** (73.47) and **Daulton Varsho** (68.37), both of whom capitalize on high-upside matchups against pitchers with xERAs above 4.50. **Lawrence Butler** benefits immensely from a favorable park environment and a massive 16% lift from the SP skill profile, making him our highest-rated asset despite a projected lower-third batting order. Similarly, **Daulton Varsho** is a Tier-1 start; his ability to mitigate the \"Elite Hold\" risk against opposing backstops provides a stable floor, while his 13% SP skill bonus against Rea creates the optimal runway for production. **Bryson Stott** (63.77) rounds out our elite tier, where the combination of a slow opposing delivery\u2014granting him a +4% SB opportunity edge\u2014and a +13% platoon adjustment makes him a locked-in MI start despite facing a tough right-handed arm in Freddy Peralta.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was the exclusion of **Drew Gilbert** (39.49), who fell just shy of our \"Zebras Floor\" of 40.0. While **Drew Gilbert** maintained a decent platoon edge, the combination of a daunting matchup against Meyer and a bottom-order slot resulted in a score that couldn't justify a start over the higher-ceiling profiles in our outfield. Elsewhere, we continue to monitor the status of **Taylor Ward** (46.10); while he carries a respectable score, he faces a significant hurdle today in Yoshinobu Yamamoto. His inclusion remains precarious, as the SP skill penalty is substantial, and his viability is only salvaged by a favorable wind-out condition and a +10% boost from his lead-off spot. We are also tracking **Cal Raleigh** (55.51) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (59.39) as their status remains \"Lineup Pending,\" which introduces a layer of volatility to our roster floor should their projected lineup slots shift downward before first pitch.",
